    "https://careers.uh.edu/jobs/adjunct-security-management-houston-texas-united-states-974b4868-6266-4053-91f1-7a11701fe4b3": {
      "title": "Adjunct, Security Management",
      "description": "Adjunct, Security Management - Houston, Texas, United States Adjunct, Security Management - Houston, Texas, United States Kiosk mode Adjunct, Security Management 497128 Part-Time Fin, Inf Sys, Econ & Risk Mgmt Department : Fin, Inf Sys, Econ & Risk Mgmt Salary : $4,500.00 (Commensurate with Experience/Education) Description : Adjunct Faculty — Master of Security Management Program Part-Time Adjunct Faculty Position — Online Graduate Program Position Type Part-time, adjunct faculty position (course-by-course appointment; no guarantee of teaching load) Department Finance, Information Systems, Economics and Risk Management (FISER) Location Downtown Houston campus, with courses delivered fully online Salary Commensurate with experience, credentials, and course level POSITION SUMMARY The Marilyn Davies College of Business at the University of Houston-Downtown is building its adjunct faculty position for the Master of Security Management (MSM) program. The MSM prepares working professionals to identify threats; both cyber and traditional and to lead risk-based security decisions at the enterprise level. We are looking for practitioners and educators who can bring that same judgment into the graduate classroom: people who have run a security program, managed a crisis, briefed an executive team, or built a risk function, and who want to help the next generation of security leaders do the same. Adjunct appointments are made on a course-by-course basis according to enrollment and program need. Being added to the position does not guarantee a teaching assignment in any given term; faculty are contacted as sections become available in their area of expertise. ABOUT THE PROGRAM The MSM is a fully online graduate program designed for working security, risk, and compliance professionals. Students complete a core curriculum spanning executive leadership and communication, crisis and emergency communication, enterprise security management, risk analysis, the legal environment of the security executive, and cybercrime and computer forensics, with the option to pursue a Cybersecurity Graduate Certificate pathway alongside the degree. Graduates move into roles in corporate and business security, cybersecurity management, and enterprise risk leadership across industry and government. RESPONSIBILITIES Teach assigned graduate courses online (and in hybrid or other approved formats as offered), meeting all courses, department, and university requirements and deadlines. Bring professional experience into the classroom through relevant cases, exercises, and applied assignments, while maintaining appropriate confidentiality and professional boundaries. Create an engaging, rigorous learning environment suited to working adult professionals. Grade students work promptly, provide substantive feedback, and document attainment of course learning outcomes. Use the university's learning management system (Canvas) and other approved instructional technology effectively. Stay current in the subject matter taught and participate in required orientation, training, assessment, and course-coordination activities. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Master's degree or higher in security management, cybersecurity, information systems, risk management, or a closely related field. Academic preparation is appropriate to the assigned course, normally a graduate degree in the discipline, or at least 18 graduate semester credit hours in the teaching field. Exceptional professional achievement may be considered in place of the credit-hour standard, consistent with university credentialing policy. Professional experience and subject-matter expertise sufficient to teach the assigned course at the graduate level. Ability to communicate clearly and work effectively with a diverse population of graduate students, faculty, and staff. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Three or more years of progressive experience in enterprise security, cybersecurity, risk management, compliance, investigations, or emergency/crisis management. Recent leadership, consulting, or operational experience in corporate, government, military, healthcare, energy, financial-services, or other regulated or critical-infrastructure environments — Houston's energy sector. Hands-on experience designing, running, or improving an enterprise security program across governance, risk, compliance, physical security, cybersecurity, or business continuity. Prior teaching, executive-education, corporate-training, or mentoring experience with adult professional audiences. Experience teaching online or hybrid courses and working within a learning management system. Current professional certification relevant to the assigned course — for example CISSP, CISM, CRISC, CISA, or Security+. Active professional engagement through practice, consulting, publications, conference presentations, standards work, or leadership in a professional association. REPRESENTATIVE COURSES & TEACHING AREAS Adjunct assignments are drawn from the MSM core and elective curriculum, including: MGT 6301 — Leadership, Motivation and Communications for Security Executives COMM 6306 — Crisis and Emergency Communication SM 6360 — Enterprise Security Management SM 6362 — Risk Analysis and Abatement SM 6364 — Legal Environment of the Security Executive SM 6365 — Cyber Crime and Computer Forensics Elective and certificate coursework in cybersecurity governance, physical and personnel security, business continuity, threat and intelligence analysis, and emerging areas such as AI governance and third-party risk. APPLICATION MATERIALS Applicants should be prepared to submit the following through the university application system: Curriculum vitae or resume Cover letter identifying areas of teaching expertise and relevant industry experience Unofficial graduate transcripts (official transcripts required if selected) Evidence of professional certifications, licenses, or teaching/training experience, where applicable Faculty inquiries about this posting may be directed to Daya Nand, Ph.D., Assistant Professor, Security Management Program, Marilyn Davies College of Business, University of Houston-Downtown. All positions in the University of Houston System are security-sensitive and require a criminal background check. All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply. Apply now First Name (required) 7d71e11e Last Name (required) 9d7da639 Email (required) 5a7023d8 Cell number 34f4d51f 244 results found No results found Afghanistan (+93) Åland Islands (+358) Albania (+355) Algeria (+213) American Samoa (+1) Andorra (+376) Angola (+244) Anguilla (+1) Antigua & Barbuda (+1) Argentina (+54) Armenia (+374) Aruba (+297) Ascension Island (+247) Australia (+61) Austria (+43) Azerbaijan (+994) Bahamas (+1) Bahrain (+973) Bangladesh (+880) Barbados (+1) Belarus (+375) Belgium (+32) Belize (+501) Benin (+229) Bermuda (+1) Bhutan (+975) Bolivia (+591) Bosnia & Herzegovina (+387) Botswana (+267) Brazil (+55) British Indian Ocean Territory (+246) British Virgin Islands (+1) Brunei (+673) Bulgaria (+359) Burkina Faso (+226) Burundi (+257) Cambodia (+855) Cameroon (+237) Canada (+1) Cape Verde (+238) Caribbean Netherlands (+599) Cayman Islands (+1) Central African Republic (+236) Chad (+235) Chile (+56) China (+86) Christmas Island (+61) Cocos (Keeling) Islands (+61) Colombia (+57) Comoros (+269) Congo - Brazzaville (+242) Congo - Kinshasa (+243) Cook Islands (+682) Costa Rica (+506) Côte d’Ivoire (+225) Croatia (+385) Cuba (+53) Curaçao (+599) Cyprus (+357) Czechia (+420) Denmark (+45) Djibouti (+253) Dominica (+1) Dominican Republic (+1) Ecuador (+593) Egypt (+20) El Salvador (+503) Equatorial Guinea (+240) Eritrea (+291) Estonia (+372) Eswatini (+268) Ethiopia (+251) Falkland Islands (Islas Malvinas) (+500) Faroe Islands (+298) Fiji (+679) Finland (+358) France (+33) French Guiana (+594) French Polynesia (+689) Gabon (+241) Gambia (+220) Georgia (+995) Germany (+49) Ghana (+233) Gibraltar (+350) Greece (+